  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through next Monday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: The increase in low level moisture the\npast day or two in combination with cool air advection early this\nmorning has produced pockets of low stratus overnight, and may \nresult in patchy fog through around 8AM this morning. Otherwise \nmostly sunny today. The upper level low currently centered over \nnorthern Idaho and the intermountain west will exit eastward the \nrest of today and tonight which will result in much lighter winds\nacross the CWA. Winds today will generally be in the 5 to 10 mph \nrange with gusts at or below 20 mph. Low temperatures tonight into\nTuesday morning are now expected to be a little warmer than \nprevious guidance suggested, with the threat of frost mainly \nlimited to valleys of Elko County. High pressure rebuilds across \nNV beginning on Tuesday with temperatures trending warmer through \nmid-week. Mid-level moisture is expected to push up into Central \nNevada Wednesday afternoon and expand into portions on Northern \nNevada by Thursday afternoon. Weak instability in combination with\nthe increased mid-level moisture may result in a few thunderstorms\non Thursday, with current guidance giving a 10% chance of TS\nacross Eureka County and the southern half of Elko County into\nearly Thursday evening. The next upper level low approaches NV \nfrom the northwest on Friday. Southwesterly flow aloft ahead of \nthe approaching system will help keep most of the region dry on \nFriday, however mid-level moisture and instability near Great \nBasin National Park may be enough for isolated showers and \nthunderstorms Friday afternoon and early evening. \n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: No changes were made to the\ncurrent NBM forecast. There is high confidence cooler than normal\ntemperatures today and tonight. \n\nHigh confidence in a warming trend Tuesday through mid-week, with\nlow confidence in thunderstorms developing Thursday afternoon.",
